Neon changes to the port: a container tab restored on a WebUI page (new tab
page, settings, history) gets a SiteInstance with no site, fixed to its
container's partition, as noopener windows do, instead of the default
partition Brave restores it in. A restored about:blank anchors the partition
on a site from the tab's own history, since a blank page committed in a
SiteInstance without a site trips a BrowsingInstance CHECK on the next
navigation.

Neon also lets spaces pick the container of new tabs that have no opener,
source page or explicit container (Ctrl+T, the new tab button, links from
other apps): GetStoragePartitionConfigToInherit asks a provider set with
SetNewTabContainerProvider, where Brave master consults its "open new tabs
in" setting (brave-core #40693).

Neon (M1b) also opens tabs that Chromium creates by URL on behalf of a
container in that container (ScopedNewTabStoragePartition): the tabs of a
saved tab group reopened from tab restore, which Brave opens in the default
partition (brave-core #58219), and the new half of a split. An extension page
opened as a new tab (chrome.tabs.create, runtime.openOptionsPage) stays in the
default partition, where its extension keeps its storage, whatever space
shows.

